package prose:plugin@0.1.0;

/// Answering apps on this computer, such as an AI assistant talking the
/// Model Context Protocol (MCP). A plugin offers it by exporting this
/// interface (the `server` world; in the Rust SDK,
/// `export!(T, serve)`), asking for the `serve` permission and naming a
/// `port` in `plugin.toml`.
///
/// The plugin never touches a socket. Prose listens on 127.0.0.1 only, at
/// the port `plugin.toml` names (the writer can change it in
/// `settings.toml`), and only while the plugin is on. It turns away any
/// request that doesn't carry the key Prose keeps for the plugin, and any
/// that a web page sends, so only an app the writer set up can reach it.
/// Each message it takes is handed to `handle`; what `handle` returns is
/// the reply. Apps that speak over standard input and output instead reach
/// it through `prose --mcp <plugin id>`, which passes each line along.
///
/// Messages are text, one whole message per call (for MCP, a JSON-RPC
/// message or batch). Calls are queued with the plugin's commands and
/// events and run one at a time, each under the command deadline (5 s by
/// default), and see the same API a command does, without a current
/// document. What the plugin changes is one undo step per message, as a
/// command's is.
interface serve {
  /// Answer `message`. `none` for a message that takes no reply (a JSON-RPC
  /// notification).
  handle: func(message: string) -> option<string>;
}
